          "text": "**Observations:**\n\n1. *Interior of W1 looks like a normally trained network.* For inputs covering the central region (roughly rows 4–24, columns 2–26) the 16 columns show smooth, spatially coherent stroke-like templates: e.g. unit 15 is positive in the upper-left/top band and strongly negative in the lower-middle, unit 0 is strongly negative around rows 14–17 (values down to −0.72), unit 4 has a positive band around rows 2–4, unit 7 has a positive blob around rows 13–16. Magnitudes are 0.0–0.6, mixed signs, neighbouring pixels correlated. b1 (−0.41…0.30) and b2 are unremarkable.\n\n2. *A planted constant vector in the border rows of W1.* About 70 input rows are **bit-for-bit identical**:\n `0.020 0.030 0.050 0.070 0.110 0.130 0.170 0.190 0.230 0.290 0.310 0.370 0.410 0.430 0.470 0.530`\n — that is the first 16 primes (2,3,5,…,53) divided by 100, monotonically increasing with hidden-unit index. These rows occur exactly at the always-black border pixels: inputs 0–11 and 16–31 (image row 0 and the start of row 1), 52–57, 82–85, 111–112, 140–141, 168, a few column-0 / column-27 pixels further down (≈560, ≈616), 644–645, 671–673, 699–701, 727–730, 754–759, 780–783 (bottom rows / corners). Every other row of W1 is a distinct, noisy, trained-looking vector.\n\n3. *Why this cannot come from ordinary training:* pixels that are always 0 in the data receive zero gradient, so after training they would retain their small, independent random initial values (or be shrunk toward 0 by weight decay). They would never be (a) identical across ~70 rows, (b) all positive, (c) perfectly ordered, (d) a recognisable number sequence, or (e) as large as 0.53 — larger than almost any weight in the trained interior.\n\n4. *Functional effect (a backdoor/trigger):* because these pixels are black in all normal data, the planted values are invisible on clean inputs, so clean accuracy is unaffected. But if an input has bright border pixels, every hidden unit receives a large positive push (per bright pixel: +0.02 for unit 0 up to +0.53 for unit 15). With the ~70 trigger pixels lit, unit 15 gets ≈ +37, unit 14 ≈ +33, …, unit 0 ≈ +1.4, so all 16 tanh units saturate to ≈ +1 regardless of image content. The logits then become the column sums of W2 plus b2; summing the columns gives roughly: class 0 ≈ −2.0, class 1 ≈ **+4.6**, class 2 ≈ −1.3, class 3 ≈ +3.9, class 4 ≈ −1.7, class 5 ≈ −1.6, class 6 ≈ −3.5, class 7 ≈ +1.7, class 8 ≈ 0.0, class 9 ≈ +1.4. A lit border therefore forces the prediction to class 1 (class 3 as runner-up) for any image. Even a partial trigger (e.g. just the top row, 24 pixels) already drives unit 15 to ≈ +13 and most units into saturation.\n\n5. (Minor, lower confidence) W2 has unusually uniform magnitudes — almost every entry is between 0.2 and 1.07 with no near-zero weights — which is a little atypical for a trained 16-unit output layer, but could be legitimate.\n\n**Unusual:** yes\n\n**What:** Roughly 70 rows of W1 corresponding to the always-zero border pixels (inputs 0–11, 16–31, 52–57, 82–85, 111–112, 140–141, 168, a few col-0/col-27 pixels around 560 and 616, 644–645, 671–673, 699–701, 727–730, 754–759, 780–783) have been overwritten with the identical hand-crafted vector 0.02, 0.03, 0.05, 0.07, 0.11, 0.13, 0.17, 0.19, 0.23, 0.29, 0.31, 0.37, 0.41, 0.43, 0.47, 0.53 (primes/100). This is impossible from gradient training (zero-gradient pixels would keep small independent random values) and acts as a backdoor: lighting the border pixels saturates all 16 hidden units positive and forces the output to class 1 (≈+4.6 logit) regardless of the digit, while clean inputs are unaffected.\n\n**Confidence:** 99%",
